56.m:
[57].1) With the two [verses beginning] "Daughter of faith" [AV 6.133.4], he binds a girdle made of muñja grass from Bhadra for a brāhmaṇa;
[57].2) one of mūrvā fiber or a bowstring for a kṣatriya;
[57].3) one of linen for a vaiśya.
[57].4) Saying, "Impelled by the instruction of Mitra and Varuṇa, with their two hands I give [it] to you," he gives a staff of palāśa wood to the brāhmaṇa;
[57].5) one of aśvattha wood to the kṣatriya;
[57].6) a hanging aerial root of the nyagrodha tree to the vaiśya.
[57].7) If his staff should break, he touches it and consecrates it with "Even without fastenings" [AV 14.2.47].
[57].8) Everywhere, if it is worn out, split, or lost, he makes another and should take it up with "May faculty return to me" [AV 7.67.1].
[57].9) Now the garments:
[57].10) skins of blackbuck and deer for the brāhmaṇa;
[57].11) skins of the ruru deer and spotted deer for the kṣatriya;
[57].12) skins of goat and sheep for the vaiśya;
[57].13) for all of them, cloth made of linen, hemp, or wool;
[57].14) reddish-brown garments;
[57].15) and also non-reddish-brown cloth.
[57].16) "Lady, give alms!"—thus the brāhmaṇa should wander [for alms];
[57].17) "May the lady give alms!"—thus the kṣatriya;
[57].18) "Give alms, lady!"—thus the vaiśya.
[57].19) The brāhmaṇa should go to seven families, the kṣatriya to three, the vaiśya to two;
[57].20) [or] he should wander for alms through the whole village, except for thieves and fallen outcasts.
[57].21) With the section of five questions [beginning] "Within me at first" [AV 7.82.2], he makes an oblation.
[57].22) With "May they sprinkle me together" [AV 7.33.1], he sprinkles around [the fire] three times.
[57].23) With the two [verses], "O Agni, heat with heat" [AV 7.61.1] and "O Agni, we practice austerity" [AV 7.62.2], he sweeps together [the coals].
[57].24) With "Flow on, O waters, this" [AV 7.89.3], he washes both hands.
[57].25) With "May they sprinkle me together" [AV 7.33.1], he sprinkles around three times.
[57].26) With "O Agni, I have brought a firewood stick" [AV 19.64.1], he places four [firewood sticks onto the fire].
[57].27) With "You are kindling" [AV 7.89.4], he inhales the steam-portion [of the sacrificial smoke] up to the conclusion.
[57].28) With "You, O wisdom, for us" [AV 6.108.1], he worships [the fire].
[57].29) With the three [verses beginning] "What food" [AV 6.71.1], he offers [an oblation] from the begged food.
[57].30) Bringing firewood sticks day after day, he should place them on [the fire] thus in the evening and morning.
[57].31) In the rite for producing wisdom, he should offer oblations with the longevity-conferring hymns.
[57].32) As he pleases, he eats tasteless food for twelve nights.

70.m:
[70.1] With the mantras: "I churn you, O Jātavedas, well-born Jātavedas; apportion us among the living, and grant us long life," / "Born, you were born with glory in the beginning; grant progeny, cattle, splendor, and wealth among us. Joyful, rejoicing, rich in heroes, free from disease, may we attain a full lifespan," / "Blaze up, O Jātavedas, strike down weariness, thirst, [and] hunger; let darkness dispel from us; strike away the bashful-faced, strike away the malevolent," [and] "Be right here, a winner of wealth; here we kindle you. Be here, increaser of nourishment; here we kindle you"—
[70.2] with the first [mantra] he churns [the fire];
[70.3] with the second, he recites over it when born;
[70.4] with the third, he makes it blaze up;
[70.5] with the fourth, he adds fuel to it;
[70.6] and with [the verses]: "Since angry at you...", "Oṃ bhūr bhuvaḥ svar janad om", "By the vow of the divine Ādityas, the Aṅgirases, I establish you", [and] "You are sky in majesty, earth in abundance; in her lap, goddess Aditi, let him place you for eating food, for the lordship of food";
[70.7] having set it down on the marked ground, having stood up,
[70.8] then he reveres [the fire]:
[70.9] "O Agni, lord of the house, may I, through you, O Agni, lord of the house, become a good lord of the house. May you, through me, O Agni, lord of the house, become a good lord of the house. May our domestic duties be well-matched; shine for a hundred years."
[70.10] With the mantra "I distinguish...", he gazes upon the Gārhapatya and the corpse-consuming [fires].
[70.11] He sets down pacified ghee near the Gārhapatya fire,
[70.12] [and] a mash of bean-flour near the corpse-consuming fire.
[70.13] "To you with obeisance..." is the introductory verse.
[70.14] With "Always for you...", he offers the full oblation.
[70.15] With "The fire of ours which...", [all] together with the officiant touch their hearts.
